{
  "id": "he-7",
  "lang": "he",
  "title": "The article: “the”",
  "steps": [
    {
      "step": "hear",
      "intro": "“The” isn't a separate word in Hebrew: it is הַ, fixed to the front of the noun. Listen for ha- (and sometimes he-).",
      "cards": [
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "👑", "text": "הַמֶּלֶךְ", "say": "ha-ME-lekh", "en": "the king" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🏠", "text": "הַבַּיִת", "say": "ha-BA-yit", "en": "the house" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "☀️", "text": "הַיּוֹם", "say": "ha-YOM", "en": "the day; today" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🌍", "text": "הָאָרֶץ", "say": "ha-A-rets", "en": "the earth, the land" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🏙️", "text": "הָעִיר", "say": "ha-IR", "en": "the city" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "🌑", "text": "הַחֹשֶׁךְ", "say": "ha-CHO-shekh", "en": "the darkness" },
        { "type": "scene", "picture": "⛰️", "text": "הֶהָרִים", "say": "he-ha-RIM", "en": "the mountains" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "notice",
      "cards": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "הַ and a doubled letter",
          "body": [
            "The article is ה with patah, and it doubles the first letter of its noun with a dot: מֶלֶךְ → הַמֶּלֶךְ, the king. Look for both: הַ, and the dot just after it.",
            "Hebrew has no word for “a”: מֶלֶךְ alone is “a king” or just “king”. With הַ it is “the king”."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "t": "מֶלֶךְ", "say": "ME-lekh", "en": "a king" },
            { "t": "הַמֶּלֶךְ", "say": "ha-ME-lekh", "en": "the king: הַ plus a doubled מּ" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "letters": true,
          "title": "When the first letter can't be doubled",
          "body": [
            "The throat letters (lesson 4) can't be doubled, so the article changes instead:",
            "Before א ע ר, the patah stretches to qamets: הָאָרֶץ, הָעִיר. Before ה ח, it stays הַ, and the doubling is just understood: הַחֹשֶׁךְ. Before an unstressed הָ, חָ or עָ, it becomes הֶ: הֶהָרִים.",
            "So “the” can look like הַ, הָ or הֶ. A ה fixed to the front of a noun is your clue."
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": ["first letter", "article", "example"],
            "rows": [
              ["most letters", "הַ + dot", "הַמֶּלֶךְ"],
              ["א ע ר", "הָ", "הָעִיר"],
              ["ה ח", "הַ", "הַחֹשֶׁךְ"],
              ["unstressed הָ חָ עָ", "הֶ", "הֶחָכָם"]
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "practise",
      "cards": [
        {
          "type": "choose",
          "prompt": "What does הַבַּיִת mean?",
          "options": ["the house", "a house", "houses", "in the house"],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "הַ is “the”, and the dot in בּ is the doubling that comes with it: the house."
        },
        {
          "type": "choose",
          "prompt": "Which is “the man”? (man is אִישׁ)",
          "options": ["הָאִישׁ", "הַאִישׁ", "הֶאִישׁ"],
          "answer": 0,
          "fragments": true,
          "explain": "א can't be doubled, so the patah lengthens to qamets: הָאִישׁ."
        },
        {
          "type": "match",
          "prompt": "Match each word to its meaning.",
          "pairs": [["הָעָם", "the people"], ["הַגּוֹיִם", "the nations"], ["הַיָּם", "the sea"], ["הַשַּׁעַר", "the gate"]]
        },
        {
          "type": "parse",
          "prompt": "Describe הָעִיר.",
          "word": "הָעִיר",
          "fields": [
            { "label": "Has “the”?", "options": ["yes", "no"], "answer": "yes" },
            { "label": "Why הָ and not הַ?", "options": ["ע can't be doubled", "the noun is plural", "the noun is feminine"], "answer": "ע can't be doubled" }
          ],
          "explain": "הָעִיר, the city: the article's vowel lengthens because ע, a throat letter, can't take the doubling dot."
        },
        {
          "type": "build",
          "prompt": "Make “the king”: put הַ in front of מֶּלֶךְ.",
          "tiles": ["הַ", "מֶּלֶךְ"],
          "join": "",
          "explain": "הַ + מֶּלֶךְ = הַמֶּלֶךְ. The dot in מּ is the article's doubling."
        },
        {
          "type": "choose",
          "prompt": "How would you say “a city”?",
          "options": ["עִיר", "הָעִיר", "הַעִיר"],
          "answer": 0,
          "fragments": true,
          "explain": "Hebrew has no word for “a”: עִיר on its own is “a city”."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "read",
      "intro": "Find every “the”: ה at the front, with a dot or a long vowel after it.",
      "cards": [
        { "type": "verse", "ref": "GEN 1:4", "note": "הָאוֹר (the light) takes הָ before א; הַחֹשֶׁךְ (the darkness) keeps הַ before ח." },
        { "type": "verse", "ref": "GEN 1:1", "note": "הַשָּׁמַיִם (the heavens, with a doubled שּׁ) and הָאָרֶץ (the earth, with הָ before א)." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": "review",
      "cards": [
        {
          "type": "words",
          "items": [
            { "k": "H2022G", "t": "הַר", "say": "har", "en": "mountain, hill" },
            { "k": "H1471A", "t": "גּוֹי", "say": "goy", "en": "nation" },
            { "k": "H5892B", "t": "עִיר", "say": "ir", "en": "city, town" },
            { "k": "H2822", "t": "חֹשֶׁךְ", "say": "CHO-shekh", "en": "darkness" },
            { "k": "H1964G", "t": "הֵיכָל", "say": "hei-KHAL", "en": "temple, palace" },
            { "k": "H8179G", "t": "שַׁעַר", "say": "SHA-ar", "en": "gate" },
            { "k": "H3220G", "t": "יָם", "say": "yam", "en": "sea" },
            { "k": "H3915", "t": "לַיְלָה", "say": "LAY-lah", "en": "night" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
